Golden-hour Ocean ("Open ocean at golden hour under a physically based sky")
by gokayfem
https://github.com/gokayfem/shader-skills/blob/main/gallery/ocean.glsl
(shader-skills, commit a56e7738d3f22698e795f036b4919f0734ab621d, 2026-10-23)

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the
top of main.metal for what was changed.

The licence statements that cover this shader
---------------------------------------------

1. The repository's LICENSE file, https://github.com/gokayfem/shader-skills/blob/main/LICENSE
   (the full text is reproduced below). It begins:

       MIT License

       Copyright (c) 2026 gokayfem

2. CREDITS.md, lines 3 to 6, https://github.com/gokayfem/shader-skills/blob/main/CREDITS.md :

       All GLSL in this repository is an original implementation, released under the MIT license (see LICENSE). The
       techniques come from the published work below. Numeric constants (matrices, fit coefficients, physical
       coefficients) are facts reproduced from their sources. No third-party shader source is copied; Shadertoy shaders
       default to CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, which is incompatible with MIT.

3. The shader file's own header, gallery/ocean.glsl line 11:

       // Library code below is bundled from shader-skills (MIT): https://github.com/gokayfem/shader-skills

Provenance: the file credits no other shader author. CREDITS.md lists the
published techniques it reimplements (among them Finch, GPU Gems chapter 1, for
the sum-of-sines water; Troy Sobotka's AgX with Benjamin Wrensch's polynomial
fit, listed there as MIT; Hillaire's atmosphere constants; Gustavson's simplex
noise; Perlin and Neyret's flow noise; Jarzynski and Olano's PCG hashes) and
states that no third-party shader source was copied. Port note: the numeric
constants (matrices, fit coefficients, physical coefficients) are the facts
CREDITS.md describes.

What was changed
----------------

- mainImage and the out/inout parameters are written the Metal way, two local
  array constructors are brace lists, and locals that wave() fills in are
  initialised.
- The waves, ripples and foam run at 0.45 times the original speed
  (LW_TIME_SCALE) so the sea moves calmly behind desktop windows.
- For cost, with the same output: ZERO is 0 instead of min(iFrame, 0), and the
  wave table, the wave bounds, the sun transmittance and the zenith sky colour,
  which the original recomputed for every pixel although they never change, are
  stored as constants. The author's functions that produce them are kept in
  the file.
- scene.json: drawn at scale 0.4 and at most 10 frames a second.
- Nothing else was touched. At the author's preview size and time (640x360,
  t = 12.5, original speed) the port matches gallery/ocean.png to within 1/255
  on every pixel.
- preview.jpg is a frame rendered from this port.

